1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,400
Onycholysis — if your nail keeps lifting away from the nail bed and turning white, there's a reason.

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:14,960
Onycholysis is when the nail plate separates from the skin underneath, trapping air that turns the area white or yellow.

3
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:23,440
Three habits usually cause it: aggressive filing, over-soaking in acetone, and back-to-back gel sessions.

4
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:27,440
First, aggressive buffing before a gel manicure.

5
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:35,120
Technicians press hard to rough up the surface so gel sticks, and that pressure alone can start the lift.

6
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:39,040
Ask for gentle buffing, or skip heavy sanding.

7
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:44,400
Second, soaking nails in acetone too long to strip old gel.

8
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:52,880
Long soaks dissolve the oils holding the nail together, and picking at the edges peels off healthy layers with the polish.

9
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:56,800
Have a technician file the gel down thin instead.

10
00:00:57,120 --> 00:01:01,680
Third, booking gel sessions back to back with no break.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,720
Once the plate lifts, it doesn't reattach on its own — it has to grow out, which takes months.

12
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:13,120
Leave nails bare for at least two weeks between gel sets.

13
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:19,280
If the lifting spreads or the nail changes color, get it checked by a dermatologist.
